\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Selected backgrounds and invariants}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Can geometry be induced instead of supplied? Edge weights set the
stiffness of field differences and may encode geometric information;
they are not universally distances. Comparing choices through an
[induced action](fdip-005Q) first requires keeping the graph data
distinct from the choice of which field to retain.
A retained field need not consist of named vertex coordinates.
It may be a symmetry sector, or another specified subspace of the
vertex fields. An orthogonal projector records this choice relative
to the counting metric. Integrating out its orthogonal complement
produces a Gaussian law on the chosen subspace.}

\p{The graph and its selector remain separate data. A graph relabeling
must transport both, while an arbitrary orthogonal comparison of
precisions can forget incidence. The examples below compare three
selectors on one four-cycle and two rank-four selectors on a quaternion
Cayley graph. Their retained spectra separate choices that the
unselected graph cannot distinguish. The restriction criterion then
identifies exactly when eliminating the complement changes the
compressed precision; a stable prism line shows why group symmetry
alone is insufficient. In this chapter #{\Gamma} denotes a symmetry
group and #{G=Q^{-1}} denotes the Green operator.}

